        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "The actual busy work, the file I/O, happens before this block. What\r\nui_busy_start()/stop() wrap here is a single line: emitting the \"written\"\r\nmessage (PR #29447 added the pair precisely so the cursor doesn't flicker\r\nto the cmdline while that message prints). So in this location the pair\r\ncan't be a \"Nvim is busy\" signal; its only purpose is hiding the cursor\r\nduring message output.\r\n\r\nWhen silent (silent! write / autosave), that line neither displays a\r\nmessage nor records history, so start/stop do nothing observable except\r\nhide and restore the cursor once, which is exactly the flicker. The file\r\nis already saved (statusline '+' is gone by then); additionally indicating\r\n\"I'm busy during the message\" during a silent write buys nothing.",
