            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "Tested all four combos (foot + Neovide, with and without the pair),\r\nsaving via `:nnoremap <silent> w :write<CR>`:\r\n\r\n- foot, pair removed: cursor leaves the editor and shows in the cmdline.\r\n- foot, current default: same, it still leaves the editor and shows in\r\n  the cmdline. The pair does not prevent the jump it was added for.\r\n- Neovide, pair removed: no flicker at all, and no cursor animation jump.\r\n- Neovide, current default: cursor hides and reappears (a new flicker\r\n  from the pair), still no animation jump.\r\n  \r\nEnv: NixOS 26.11pre (kernel 7.2.9, Wayland), foot 1.28.0, Neovide\r\nnightly-39+g802c67f, nvim at 326d764e3e.\r\n\r\nSo the pair misses its stated goal in the terminal, is unnecessary in\r\nNeovide either way (on current main), and adds a hide/show flicker there. Dropping the\r\ncalls, as you suggested, looks like the right move.\r\n\r\n> Do we call ui_busy_start before then?\r\n\r\nNo, nothing sets busy during the actual I/O. This is the only ui_busy_start in the whole write path, so the pair cannot be signaling busyness.",
